{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-325132",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the commute time (in minutes) of a randomly selected person. Then we have $X∼N(40,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<31.7).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>52)=0.15.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 52) & =1-P(X>52) \\\\  & =1-0.15 \\\\  & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 52) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{52-40}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{12}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{12}{1.0364} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈11.5785.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X\\le 31.7)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 31.7) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{31.7-40}{11.5785}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -0.72) \\\\  & ≈Φ(-0.72) \\\\  & =0.2358 \\\\  & ≈23.6%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $23.6%$ of commutes take less than $31.7$ minutes.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
